---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How do I view email headers?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Gmail: three-dot menu, Show original. Outlook: File, Properties, Internet headers. Apple Mail: View, Message, All Headers."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Are email headers always honest?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The bottom Received headers (closest to origin) are hardest to fake. Top headers can be injected by the sender. Authentication-Results are added by the receiving server, making them trustworthy."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the difference between From and Return-Path?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "From is what the recipient sees. Return-Path is the technical bounce address. DMARC requires alignment between them (via SPF or DKIM)."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can I trace where an email actually came from?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes: read the Received headers bottom-up. The first Received hop (bottom) is closest to the sender’s server, with its IP address."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What does X-Mailer tell me?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The software the sender used (e.g., Outlook, PHP mail, a specific ESP). Useful for identifying automated or spoofed mail."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Should I worry about headers in emails I receive?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "For suspicious emails, yes: header analysis reveals whether the claimed sender is genuine. Use our header analyzer to automate the check."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How to Read Email Headers (With Examples) | PromptDNS"
  description="how to read email headers"
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">How to Read Email Headers</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">How to Read Email Headers</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Email headers contain the full delivery story: who sent the message, through which servers, and whether authentication passed. Reading them takes practice; this guide annotates a real header.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The headers that matter</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60"><table class="pdns-table"><tbody><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">From</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The display address the recipient sees</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Can be spoofed: verify with Authentication-Results</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Authentication-Results</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">SPF, DKIM, DMARC verdicts from the receiving server</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Your deliverability report card</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Received</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The chain of servers, bottom (origin) to top (your inbox)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Read bottom-up to trace the path</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">DKIM-Signature</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The signing details including selector (s=) and domain (d=)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Use the selector to look up the public key</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Return-Path</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The envelope sender (bounce address)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Should align with the From domain for DMARC</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Red flags to look for</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li>spf=fail or dkim=fail in Authentication-Results.</li><li>From address that doesn’t match the Return-Path domain (alignment failure).</li><li>Received hops from unexpected countries or unknown providers.</li><li>Missing DKIM-Signature header entirely.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I view email headers?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Gmail: three-dot menu, Show original. Outlook: File, Properties, Internet headers. Apple Mail: View, Message, All Headers.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Are email headers always honest?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The bottom Received headers (closest to origin) are hardest to fake. Top headers can be injected by the sender. Authentication-Results are added by the receiving server, making them trustworthy.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is the difference between From and Return-Path?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">From is what the recipient sees. Return-Path is the technical bounce address. DMARC requires alignment between them (via SPF or DKIM).</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I trace where an email actually came from?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: read the Received headers bottom-up. The first Received hop (bottom) is closest to the sender’s server, with its IP address.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What does X-Mailer tell me?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The software the sender used (e.g., Outlook, PHP mail, a specific ESP). Useful for identifying automated or spoofed mail.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Should I worry about headers in emails I receive?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">For suspicious emails, yes: header analysis reveals whether the claimed sender is genuine. Use our header analyzer to automate the check.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/email-header-analyzer/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Email header analyzer →</a></li><li><a href="/dmarc/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC guide →</a></li><li><a href="/spf-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF records →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
